/**
 * @file Foundation.dox
 *
 * @defgroup Foundation Foundation
 *
 * @brief Core documentation topic for the Foundation framework.
 *
 * Foundation is a modular C++ framework designed for embedded and
 * desktop software development.
 *
 * The framework provides reusable software components organized into
 * independent modules. Each module focuses on a specific domain such as
 * mathematics, time handling, containers, scheduling, or compile-time
 * utilities.
 *
 * @section Foundation_Overview Overview
 *
 * Foundation is intended to provide a stable base layer for projects
 * that need reusable, lightweight, and portable C++ components.
 *
 * The main design goal is to support both resource-constrained embedded
 * systems and desktop environments without forcing unnecessary
 * dependencies on the user.
 *
 * Foundation can be used as:
 *
 * - A standalone utility library.
 * - A dependency for higher-level frameworks.
 * - A base layer for embedded applications.
 * - A shared foundation for multiple projects.
 *
 * @section Foundation_DesignGoals Design Goals
 *
 * Foundation is designed around the following principles:
 *
 * - Embedded-first design.
 * - Desktop compatibility.
 * - Modular architecture.
 * - Stable public interfaces.
 * - Minimal runtime overhead.
 * - Minimal dependency on the C++ standard library.
 * - Optional module selection through the build system.
 * - Clear separation between public API and internal structure.
 *
 * @section Foundation_ModuleArchitecture Module Architecture
 *
 * Foundation is organized into modules. Each module exposes a clear
 * public interface and may contain smaller submodules.
 *
 * @code
 * Foundation
 * ├── TypeTraits
 * ├── Math
 * ├── Time
 * ├── Containers
 * ├── Functional
 * ├── Scheduling
 * └── Utils
 * @endcode
 *
 * Each module is documented as a Doxygen topic under this main
 * Foundation topic.
 *
 * @section Foundation_Modules Modules
 *
 * The main Foundation modules are:
 *
 * - @ref Foundation_TypeTraits
 * - @ref Foundation_Math
 * - @ref Foundation_Time
 * - @ref Foundation_Containers
 * - @ref Foundation_Functional
 * - @ref Foundation_Scheduling
 * - @ref Foundation_Utils
 *
 * @section Foundation_TypeTraitsModule Type Traits Module
 *
 * The Type Traits module provides compile-time type utilities.
 *
 * This module is intended to provide lightweight template
 * metaprogramming support, especially for embedded environments where
 * depending on the full C++ standard library may not be desirable.
 *
 * @section Foundation_MathModule Math Module
 *
 * The Math module provides mathematical utilities and data structures.
 *
 * Typical components include:
 *
 * - Arithmetic helpers.
 * - Ratios.
 * - Complex numbers.
 * - Fixed-size matrices.
 * - Dynamic matrices.
 * - Matrix operations.
 *
 * @section Foundation_TimeModule Time Module
 *
 * The Time module provides abstractions for representing and working
 * with time-related values.
 *
 * Typical components include:
 *
 * - Frequency.
 * - Period.
 * - Duration.
 * - Tick.
 * - TimePoint.
 * - Clock.
 *
 * @section Foundation_ContainersModule Containers Module
 *
 * The Containers module provides reusable data structures.
 *
 * This module is intended for arrays, buffers, queues, lists, or other
 * storage abstractions that are useful across embedded and desktop
 * projects.
 *
 * @section Foundation_FunctionalModule Functional Module
 *
 * The Functional module provides callable and callback utilities.
 *
 * This module is intended for lightweight callback binding, function
 * abstraction, delegates, and related utilities.
 *
 * @section Foundation_SchedulingModule Scheduling Module
 *
 * The Scheduling module provides utilities for managing task execution.
 *
 * This module is intended for periodic tasks, one-shot tasks, deferred
 * execution, and simple embedded-friendly scheduling systems.
 *
 * @section Foundation_UtilsModule Utils Module
 *
 * The Utils module contains general-purpose utilities that do not yet
 * belong to a more specific module.
 *
 * This module should remain small. If a utility grows into a clear
 * domain, it should eventually be moved into its own module.
 *
 * @section Foundation_DependencyModel Dependency Model
 *
 * Foundation modules should depend only on modules that are lower-level
 * or more general.
 *
 * A typical dependency direction is:
 *
 * @code
 * TypeTraits
 *      |
 *      v
 * Math
 *      |
 *      v
 * Time
 *      |
 *      v
 * Scheduling
 * @endcode
 *
 * Higher-level modules may depend on lower-level modules, but
 * lower-level modules should avoid depending on higher-level ones.
 *
 * @section Foundation_PublicApi Public API
 *
 * The public API of Foundation is exposed through top-level headers.
 *
 * For user-facing includes, see:
 *
 * - @ref FoundationTopLevelHeaders
 *
 * Application code should prefer the top-level public headers instead
 * of including deeply nested internal headers directly.
 *
 * @section Foundation_BuildSystem Build System
 *
 * Foundation uses CMake as its main build system.
 *
 * The build system is responsible for:
 *
 * - Enabling or disabling modules.
 * - Selecting the target platform.
 * - Exporting public headers.
 * - Generating static libraries.
 * - Generating documentation.
 * - Preparing embedded distributions.
 *
 * @section Foundation_TargetPlatforms Target Platforms
 *
 * Foundation is intended to support:
 *
 * - Desktop platforms.
 * - Arduino-style environments.
 * - PSoC Creator projects.
 * - STM32 projects.
 * - Other C++11-capable embedded targets.
 *
 * @section Foundation_Style Coding Style
 *
 * Foundation follows these general conventions:
 *
 * - PascalCase for class names.
 * - PascalCase for public function names.
 * - Nested namespaces.
 * - Explicit module ownership.
 * - Small focused headers.
 * - Minimal hidden dependencies.
 * - Embedded-friendly implementations.
 *
 * @section Foundation_Namespaces Namespaces
 *
 * All Foundation code should live inside the main Foundation namespace.
 *
 * Module namespaces are placed inside Foundation:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * namespace Foundation {
 *     namespace Math {
 *     }
 *
 *     namespace Time {
 *     }
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section Foundation_DocumentationLayout Documentation Layout
 *
 * Foundation documentation is organized into:
 *
 * - The main Foundation topic.
 * - Module topics.
 * - Submodule topics.
 * - Public API pages.
 * - Build guide pages.
 * - Example documentation.
 * - Class and file documentation.
 *
 * The main Foundation topic should describe the framework as a whole.
 * Module-specific documentation should live in the corresponding module
 * documentation files.
 *
 * @section Foundation_Versioning Versioning
 *
 * Foundation follows semantic versioning:
 *
 * @code
 * MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH
 * @endcode
 *
 * Breaking public API changes should increment the major version.
 * Backward-compatible features should increment the minor version.
 * Fixes should increment the patch version.
 *
 * @section Foundation_RelatedPages Related Pages
 *
 * Useful related documentation pages:
 *
 * - @ref FoundationTopLevelHeaders
 * - @ref FoundationBuildGuide
 * - @ref Foundation_Examples
 */